  "query_preview": "..Water vapor satellite imagery show the upper-level \nlow pressure centered over central Arizona and moving eastward, \nwith moisture from the remnants of Polo now mostly being lifted\ninto eastern New Mexico and western Texas. Shower/thunderstorm \ndevelopment is expected this morning and potentially into the\nearly afternoon hours of eastern Arizona before coming to an end.\nA Flood Watch for northern and eastern Arizona will likely be \nallowed to expire at 5 AM MST this morning as rainfall intensity\nand duration is expected to be decrease through the day. The main\nconcern for today into early Thursday will be river flooding,\nespecially along the Little Colorado River as surface water drain\nwithin the basins.\n\nTemperatures will remain quite cool through Wednesday given the \nrainfall and trough passage. Thursday morning will be quite cool \nin the wake of the trough with clearing skies and some high \nelevations cold spots may drop close to freezing.\n\nThe remainder of the week features high pressure building across\nthe southwest, resulting in a drier period with near average\ntemperatures. The dry weather looks to persist into next week.",
